%I A401031 #22 Oct 21 2026 00:02:03
%S A401031 1,1,1,2,3,3,6,10,10,20,35,35,70,126,126,252,462,462,924,1716,1716,
%T A401031 3432,6435,6435,12870,24310,24310,48620,92378,92378,184756,352716,
%U A401031 352716,705432,1352078,1352078,2704156,5200300,5200300,10400600,20058300,20058300,40116600
%N A401031 Number of compositions of n using 1's and 2's such that the number of 1's and 2's differ by 0 or 1.
%C A401031 Starting from a(2), if we group the sequence as a series of triplets (1, 2, 3), (3, 6, 10) and so forth, these triplets correspond to the three numbers starting from the i-th position (i > 0) in the (i + 1)th diagonal of the Pascal's triangle.
%H A401031 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A401031/b401031.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..1000</a>
%F A401031 For n > 0, a(n) = (A008611(n - 1) + A002264(n + 1))!/(A008611(n - 1)! * A002264(n + 1)!).
%F A401031 a(n) = A001405(floor((1+2*n)/3)). - _Alois P. Heinz_, Oct 14 2026
%e A401031 a(4) = 3 because 4 has 3 compositions (1 + 1 + 2), (1 + 2 + 1) and (2 + 1 + 1) such that the number of 1's and 2's differ at most by 1.
%t A401031 A401031[n_] := Binomial @@ Quotient[2*n + 1, {3, 6}];
%t A401031 Array[A401031, 50, 0] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 20 2026 *)
%Y A401031 A008611 and A002264 correspond to the number is 1's and 2's respectively in the composition of n such that the number of 1's and 2's differ at most by 1.
%Y A401031 Cf. A001405 (the same with duplicates removed).
%K A401031 nonn,easy,new
%O A401031 0,4
%A A401031 _Kiran Ananthpur Bacche_, Oct 14 2026
